// sh_admit — the ADMIT verb, batch admission (§4.1).
//
// Body is `varint(count)` then the raw record stream, byte for byte what
// build/wp-slice reads from a file today: repeat { i64 little-endian length,
// that many bytes } (wp_write_step.m1:96 advances the read cursor by 8+len).
// The bytes go from the frame buffer straight to a file with one mem_read_raw
// and one fs_write_bytes; they are never decoded, reordered or re-framed here,
// because §5.2 property 5 says the transport carries calls and does not decide
// about them. That is also what makes the byte-identical claim testable: an
// ADMIT of a corpus and a direct invocation over that same corpus file hand the
// writer the same bytes.
//
// ── THE SPAWN (AXVERITY_SHIM_BRIDGE_PRIMS_V1, closing R15) ──────────────────
// Round 2 stopped here because M1 could not express "start the writer". It can
// now: proc_run(program, argv) spawns, waits, and reports. Everything ABOUT the
// spawn is decided here rather than in Rust (hard limit NO_ORCHESTRATION_IN_RUST)
// — which binary, which arguments, what a given result means, and what the
// client is told. The primitive contributes no policy at all.
//
// THE WRITER PATH COMES FROM ARGV. bin/axshim passes it as argv[3], absolute,
// because this process has cd'd into the store root and a relative path would
// resolve against the store rather than the repo. The child inherits that cwd,
// which is exactly why no chdir primitive is needed (R16 stays closed): one
// process, one store, and the store is where this process already stands.
//
// ── EVERY RECORD MUST BE A TRIPLE (P9) ──────────────────────────────────────
// A record carrying a third 0x1F is not a triple with a separator in its
// target; the durable format has no escaping, so there is no reading of those
// bytes under which it is one. It used to be admitted, counted, indexed, and
// returned as a FOUR-field row that breaks the client's row parser -- the one
// thing Appendix A.5 tells a consumer cannot happen. sh_seps2 decides; the
// check rides sh_frames' existing walk and adds no second pass over the body.
//
// Refusing it HERE and not only at index time is the whole point. rt_dindex_rec
// drops it too, because a store can be written by build/wp-slice with no shim
// in the path -- but a drop at rebuild alone would leave ADMIT answering
// `ok admitted=N` for a record no query returns, which is exactly the
// admitted-and-invisible shape this intent exists to remove. Two doors, one
// rule.
//
// ── AN EMPTY BATCH IS REFUSED, NOT SPAWNED (W4) ─────────────────────────────
// A count of zero with an empty body is SELF-CONSISTENT — the body really does
// hold zero records — so the count check below passes it, and it used to be
// handed to the writer as a batch of nothing. The writer sizes its staging
// buffer from the input file and dies on mem_reserve_raw(0) ("capacity must be
// > 0, got 0"), respawns the entry in place, dies again, and never exits. This
// fn is inside proc_run waiting for that child, and this process has ONE accept
// loop, so the shim then answers nobody, forever, with the port still open and
// the process still alive: a supervisor sees health. Two bytes of body from any
// client denied service to every reader and writer until a restart.
//
// The refusal is here rather than in the writer because that is where the fact
// lives: "this batch has no records" is a property of the CALL, and refusing a
// malformed call is the same job sh_frames and the UTF-8 guard already do. The
// writer's own fragility on a zero-length input is real and is NOT fixed by
// this — it is reported, not patched, because the write path is not this
// intent's to touch. Nothing else can reach it: an empty batch is the only way
// to hand the writer an empty file, and it stops here now.
//
// It is deliberately a SEPARATE gate from the count check, not a widening of
// it. Folding "empty" into "count disagrees with body" would answer an empty
// batch with "count=0 body holds 0 records", which reads as a contradiction and
// tells the client nothing about what it did wrong.
//
// ── THE COUNT IS CHECKED AGAINST THE BODY, NOT TRUSTED ──────────────────────
// The body is self-delimiting, so how many records it holds is a fact about the
// bytes. sh_frames counts them and a disagreement is refused before the writer
// is spawned. Without this, a count one too LOW was silent data loss reported
// as success — the writer read two records out of a three-record body, wrote
// two, exited 0, and nothing was malformed for a reader to catch. See
// sh_frames.m1 for why counting is transport's job and not the store's.
//
// ── THE REPLY CARRIES THE BLOCK-ID RANGE (AXVERITY_W2_ADMIT_HANDLE_V1, G2) ──
// §4.5's two-phase write had nothing to read back: ADMIT returned a record
// count and a byte count, so a consumer needing one record to reference another
// had no assigned identity to reference. It does now, and it is PER STREAM,
// because that is what exists — the Lead Architect's ruling (a), 2026-08-26,
// amended a hard limit that had been written from a false "global, dense from
// 0" and pointed it at what §2.1 publishes instead.
//
// RELAYED, NEVER DERIVED. The spine rule is that only the block-id allocator
// mints ids and av_admit does not derive them. This fn does not scan a
// directory, does not subtract a before from an after, and does not read the
// store's layout: it reads four integers out of a file the ALLOCATOR wrote
// (lib/wp_publish_ids.m1, called from the flusher after both streams are
// sealed) and splices them into the reply. Truncated before the spawn, exactly
// like writer.err, so a stale range cannot be attached to a later admission.
//
// THE STRUCTURAL CHECK IS NOT `is the file non-empty`. A truncated or garbage
// file parses to zeros through str_to_int (arith.rs:142 returns 0 for anything
// unparseable), and 0 is a real block id in every store — the same trap
// wp_publish_ids guards on the writer's side. So the check is that the line
// really carries FOUR space-separated fields: the fourth is extracted with
// str_after, which returns "" when its delimiter is absent (str_ops.rs:143),
// so a short line yields an empty fourth field and fails.
//
// WHEN THE RANGE IS UNKNOWN THE REPLY SAYS SO AND STAYS status 0. The write
// happened — that is a fact and status 0 is the only code that carries it;
// 1, 2 and 3 all tell a client its write did not land, and a client that
// retries on any of them duplicates durable records. So the status is 0 and the
// range keys are ABSENT, replaced by `range=unavailable`. A consumer scanning
// for `structure=` finds nothing and fails loudly, rather than parsing a
// plausible zero. This branch should be unreachable once the writer publishes
// on every successful exit; it is one comparison, not infrastructure.
//
// ── THE REFUSAL NOW NAMES THE LIMIT (AXVERITY_W2_ADMIT_HANDLE_V1, G3) ───────
// A batch refused for an oversize record used to be answered with "the writer
// rejected this batch" and nothing else. The number that would let a consumer
// fix it -- max_record -- was printed by the writer to ITS OWN stdout, which
// this process does not capture and does not forward, so §4.1's "ask the
// operator" was the only way to learn a limit the server already knew. A
// refusal that withholds the reason it refused is the benign-looking end of
// UNKNOWN_MUST_NOT_LOOK_BENIGN seen from the client's side: the status is
// honest, the body is uninformative, and the consumer's only recourse is
// out-of-band.
//
// The writer now also writes that one line to .axverity-store/writer.err
// (src/hot_mem_write.m1) and this fn relays it verbatim. Relaying, not
// deriving: the limit is the writer's fact and the transport does not read
// config, re-decide policy, or reformat the number. The file is TRUNCATED here
// before the spawn, so a stale line from an earlier admission can never be
// attached to a later refusal -- an empty file after a refusal means the writer
// refused for a reason it did not name, and the reply says exactly that rather
// than inventing one.
//
// ── THE TEMP FILE IS ONE FIXED PATH, DELIBERATELY ───────────────────────────
// It was admit-<seq>.tmp, one per admission, which leaked a file per ADMIT for
// the life of the process. The bridge has no file-removal primitive and adding
// one would be a THIRD primitive, which this intent forbids outright — so the
// path is fixed instead and each admission overwrites the last. The leak is now
// exactly one file, bounded, named, and in nobody's directory fold: the
// writer's max+1 scan reads .axverity-store/structure and .axverity-store/
// payload (wp_bid_resolve.m1:27-28) and the rebuild reads structure alone
// (rt_rebuild.m1), so a file at the store root is invisible to both tiers.
// It is written only AFTER the framing check passes, so a refused batch stages
// nothing.
//
// ── THE RETURN VALUE IS ADVISORY AND IS DISCARDED ───────────────────────────
// The answer to the client is the sh_reply below; this fn's Int return is not
// it. `rc` is proc_run's raw result, and it is AMBIGUOUS on purpose-free
// grounds: a refused batch never spawns anything and so returns 0, which is
// also what a writer that succeeded returns. sh_serve_step binds it to `_a`
// and never reads it (verified in generated_sh_serve_step_xb.rs: node_51 is
// bound and has no second reference), and there is exactly one caller.
//
// Do NOT start using it without giving it an unambiguous encoding first. It is
// left as-is rather than rebuilt into a status code because adding control flow
// to a working fn for a value nobody reads is how defects get in.
//
// ── RESULT -> WIRE STATUS (§5.8, now four classes) ──────────────────────────
// proc_run reports four disjoint bands and this fn, not the bridge, decides
// what they mean:
//
//   rc = 0        the writer ran and finished          -> 0  ok
//   rc > 0        the writer ran and REJECTED the batch (e.g. a record too
//                 large for an empty block, hot_mem_write.m1's proc_exit(1))
//                 -> 1  out-of-contract: retrying these bytes will not help,
//                 which is §5.8's own test applied honestly
//   -1..-64       killed by a signal                   -> 2  transient
//   -256          the writer could not be started      -> 3  server fault
//   -257          ended, no reason given by the OS     -> 3  server fault
//
// The last class is new. Two classes could not carry three facts: "the writer
// binary is missing" is not out-of-contract (the client's bytes were fine) and
// is not transient (retrying will not help), and a client routing on status
// alone would have retried it forever. A distinguishing PREFIX was not enough —
// the class is what clients route on.
fn sh_admit(sptr: Int, hdrw: Int, plen: Int) -> Int {
  let conn = mem_read_int_raw(sptr, Int(8))
  let buf  = mem_read_int_raw(sptr, Int(16))

  // The count varint sits immediately after the opcode byte.
  let coff = int_add(hdrw, Int(1))
  let peek = mem_read_raw(buf, coff, Int(5))
  let cw   = sh_vi_width(peek, Int(0))
  let cnt  = if int_gt(cw, Int(0)) { sh_vi_val(peek, Int(0), cw) } else { int_sub(Int(0), Int(1)) }

  let roff = int_add(coff, cw)
  let rlen = int_sub(int_sub(plen, Int(1)), cw)
  let vok  = bool_and(int_gt(cw, Int(0)), int_gte(rlen, Int(0)))

  // How many records the body ACTUALLY holds. -1 if it is not cleanly framed.
  let nfr  = if vok { sh_frames(sptr, roff, rlen) } else { int_sub(Int(0), Int(1)) }
  let fok  = bool_and(vok, int_gte(nfr, Int(0)))
  let cok  = bool_and(fok, int_eq(nfr, cnt))
  // sh_frames leaves the 1-based ordinal of the first non-triple in slot 152,
  // or 0 if every record carries exactly two separators.
  let sfa  = if fok { mem_read_int_raw(sptr, Int(152)) } else { Int(0) }
  let sok  = bool_and(cok, int_eq(sfa, Int(0)))
  // ...and a batch of triples that agrees with its body still has to HAVE one.
  let bat  = bool_and(sok, int_gt(cnt, Int(0)))

  let path = Text(".axverity-store/admit.tmp")
  let wep  = Text(".axverity-store/writer.err")
  let idp  = Text(".axverity-store/admit.ids")
  let _mk  = fs_mkdir_p(Text(".axverity-store"))
  let _wr  = if bat { fs_write_bytes(path, mem_read_raw(buf, roff, rlen)) }
             else   { io_print(Text("")) }
  // Truncated BEFORE the spawn so nothing an earlier admission said can be
  // attached to this one's refusal.
  let _we  = if bat { fs_write_text(wep, Text("")) }
             else   { io_print(Text("")) }
  let _wi  = if bat { fs_write_text(idp, Text("")) }
             else   { io_print(Text("")) }

  // argv[3] is the writer; the default is only reached if bin/axshim was
  // bypassed, and it is relative on purpose so that case fails loudly at the
  // store root rather than silently running some other repo's binary.
  let wexe = argv_or(Int(3), Text("build/wp-slice"))
  let rc   = if bat { proc_run(wexe, list_of_2(path, int_to_str(cnt))) }
             else   { Int(0) }

  let good = bool_and(bat, int_eq(rc, Int(0)))
  let fail = bool_and(bat, int_gt(rc, Int(0)))
  let kill = bool_and(bat, bool_and(int_lt(rc, Int(0)), int_gt(rc, int_sub(Int(0), Int(65)))))
  let srvf = bool_and(bat, int_lte(rc, int_sub(Int(0), Int(256))))
  let nost = int_eq(rc, int_sub(Int(0), Int(256)))

  // The writer's own words, or "" if it named no reason. fs_read_text panics on
  // a missing file (io.rs:55), so the existence check is not optional.
  let werr = if fs_file_exists(wep) { fs_read_text(wep) } else { Text("") }
  let hase = int_gt(str_len(werr), Int(0))

  // The allocator's four integers: structure_first, structure_count,
  // payload_first, payload_count. A count of 0 carries first = -1, never 0.
  let sp   = chr(Int(32))
  let idt  = if fs_file_exists(idp) { fs_read_text(idp) } else { Text("") }
  let sf   = str_before(idt, sp)
  let ir0  = str_after(idt, sp)
  let sc   = str_before(ir0, sp)
  let ir1  = str_after(ir0, sp)
  let pf   = str_before(ir1, sp)
  let pc   = str_after(ir1, sp)
  let hasi = int_gt(str_len(pc), Int(0))

  // AXVERITY_W2_ADMIT_HANDLE_V1 G3, corrected at G5 — an admission makes the
  // RAM projection stale, and the condition is `bat`: A SPAWN WAS ATTEMPTED.
  //
  // IT WAS `good`, AND THAT MISSED THE CRASH. The reasoning behind `good` was
  // "a refused batch wrote nothing and staled nothing", which is true of every
  // MALFORMED refusal — those are decided above, before the writer exists — and
  // FALSE of the two refusals that come back from a writer that already ran.
  // §A.4 makes status 2 and 3 reachable only when the writer is killed by a
  // signal or cannot be started, and a signal lands wherever it lands: a killed
  // writer leaves a structurally intact PREFIX, which is durable data the
  // projection has not seen. Measured before the fix, small population, real
  // SIGKILL mid-batch: 27 durable blocks appeared and the next QUERY answered
  // status 0 with the 1 row it held from before the batch. That is the exact
  // shape T2 exists to remove, reached through the one door nobody checked, in
  // the one case where being wrong costs most.
  //
  // `bat` is deliberately WIDER than "the store changed". A writer that could
  // not be started (status 3) staled nothing, and this marks the projection
  // stale anyway. The cost is one spurious REBUILD; the alternative is
  // enumerating which failures did and did not reach disk from an exit code,
  // which is a guess this process is not in a position to make. Erring toward
  // "I do not know, so I will not answer" is the whole of
  // UNKNOWN_MUST_NOT_LOOK_BENIGN.
  let _fr = if bat { mem_write_int_raw(sptr, Int(160), Int(1)) }
            else   { io_print(Text("")) }

  let _r0 = if bool_not(vok) {
              sh_reply(conn, Int(1), Text("MALFORMED admit body: count varint did not decode"))
            } else { io_print(Text("")) }

  let _r1 = if bool_and(vok, bool_not(fok)) {
              sh_reply(conn, Int(1),
                Text("MALFORMED admit body is not a framed record stream"))
            } else { io_print(Text("")) }

  let _r2 = if bool_and(fok, bool_not(cok)) {
              sh_reply(conn, Int(1),
                str_concat(Text("MALFORMED count="),
                  str_concat(int_to_str(cnt),
                    str_concat(Text(" body holds "),
                      str_concat(int_to_str(nfr), Text(" records"))))))
            } else { io_print(Text("")) }

  let _rs = if bool_and(cok, bool_not(sok)) {
              sh_reply(conn, Int(1),
                str_concat(Text("MALFORMED record "),
                  str_concat(int_to_str(sfa),
                    Text(" is not a triple: a record must carry exactly two 0x1f separators and be valid UTF-8"))))
            } else { io_print(Text("")) }

  let _re = if bool_and(sok, bool_not(bat)) {
              sh_reply(conn, Int(1),
                Text("MALFORMED admit batch is empty: count=0 carries no records to write"))
            } else { io_print(Text("")) }

  let head = str_concat(Text("ok admitted="),
               str_concat(int_to_str(cnt),
                 str_concat(Text(" bytes="), int_to_str(rlen))))

  let _r3 = if bool_and(good, hasi) {
              sh_reply(conn, Int(0),
                str_concat(head,
                  str_concat(Text(" structure="),
                    str_concat(sf, str_concat(Text(","),
                      str_concat(sc, str_concat(Text(" payload="),
                        str_concat(pf, str_concat(Text(","), pc)))))))))
            } else { io_print(Text("")) }

  let _r8 = if bool_and(good, bool_not(hasi)) {
              sh_reply(conn, Int(0), str_concat(head, Text(" range=unavailable")))
            } else { io_print(Text("")) }

  let _r4 = if bool_and(fail, hase) {
              sh_reply(conn, Int(1),
                str_concat(Text("WRITER exit="),
                  str_concat(int_to_str(rc),
                    str_concat(Text(" the writer rejected this batch; retrying the same bytes will not help: "),
                      werr))))
            } else { io_print(Text("")) }

  let _r7 = if bool_and(fail, bool_not(hase)) {
              sh_reply(conn, Int(1),
                str_concat(Text("WRITER exit="),
                  str_concat(int_to_str(rc),
                    Text(" the writer rejected this batch and named no reason; retrying the same bytes will not help"))))
            } else { io_print(Text("")) }

  let _r5 = if kill {
              sh_reply(conn, Int(2),
                str_concat(Text("WRITER killed by signal "),
                  int_to_str(int_sub(Int(0), rc))))
            } else { io_print(Text("")) }

  let _r6 = if srvf {
              if nost {
                sh_reply(conn, Int(3), str_concat(Text("NOWRITER could not start "), wexe))
              } else {
                sh_reply(conn, Int(3),
                  Text("NOWRITER the writer ended and the OS named no exit code and no signal"))
              }
            } else { io_print(Text("")) }

  int_add(rc, Int(0))
}
